# Line endings.
#
# The launcher for macOS and Linux is a shell script, and a shell script with
# Windows line endings does not run: the interpreter reads the trailing carriage
# return as part of its own path and reports "bad interpreter: /bin/sh^M". This
# repository is written on Windows, where git would otherwise hand out CRLF on
# checkout and break that file for everyone it is meant for.
#
# So text is normalised, and the files where the ending is load-bearing are
# pinned explicitly rather than left to a guess.

* text=auto

*.command text eol=lf
*.sh      text eol=lf
*.py      text eol=lf

# Windows launchers want CRLF: a .cmd file with LF endings can mis-parse in
# cmd.exe, which is the same failure in the other direction.
*.cmd text eol=crlf
*.ps1 text eol=crlf

# Read on a Mac, so LF. TextEdit copes with either, but a file addressed to
# macOS users should not arrive full of carriage returns.
"Mac users - read this first.txt" text eol=lf

# What GitHub counts as this project's language.
#
# It said HTML, because "Global Command View.html" is a 664 kB single-file build
# and outweighed everything written by hand. A tool listed as HTML on a topic
# page reads as a web page rather than something you run, which is the opposite
# of true here: it is a Python server and a JavaScript client, and the .html is
# output rather than source.
"Global Command View.html" linguist-generated=true
docs/* linguist-documentation=true
certs/* linguist-vendored=true

# Kept in the repository, left out of the download.
#
# The social preview card is uploaded by hand in GitHub's settings and is
# referenced by nothing else. It was 963 kB of a 2.4 MB archive that every user
# downloads and no user needs. The screenshots stay, because README.md points at
# them and somebody reading it in the folder should see them.
docs/social-preview.png export-ignore

# Release notes live on the release page, not in the folder. They are written
# for somebody deciding whether to download, which is a question already
# answered by the time the archive is open, and they accumulate: every version
# leaves another one behind. CHANGELOG.md is the copy that belongs beside the
# code, and it ships.
docs/release-*.md export-ignore

# A working file for the radar colour work, kept because it records what the
# schemes actually look like, referenced by nothing.
docs/_radar-schemes.png export-ignore

# Repository plumbing. FUNDING.yml is read by GitHub and by nothing else, and
# the two git files describe how this repository is checked out - neither means
# anything inside an unpacked download.
.github export-ignore
.gitattributes export-ignore
.gitignore export-ignore
